# Development Platforms & Shared SDKs

This section turns the shared architecture into platform-specific implementation guidance. Use it to build features, apply common application foundations, and integrate SDKs through documented contracts.

**React Native with TypeScript and Redux Toolkit is our strongly preferred choice for new applications.** Native Android and iOS are supported where requirements justify them. Maintain the established stack of an existing app unless a migration is explicitly agreed.

For the wider ecosystem—including Bitrise, Sauce Labs, NowSecure, Datadog, and distribution—start with [Mobile Platform Overview](/mobile/onboarding/platform-overview/). This section focuses on application implementation rather than repeating that ecosystem guide.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep company code, SDK information, configuration, and evidence in approved systems. Never include credentials, signing material, private artifact URLs, or sensitive user data in examples or screenshots.

## 1. Find the right implementation guide

| Page | Use it to | Main outcome |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Android Standards** | Implement Kotlin features with the documented Android architecture, state, concurrency, and data patterns. | An Android feature with explicit ownership and testable boundaries. |
| **iOS Standards** | Implement Swift features with the documented iOS architecture, state, concurrency, and data patterns. | An iOS feature with deliberate actor and lifecycle boundaries. |
| **React Native Standards** | Implement TypeScript features using React Native and Redux Toolkit. | A feature with clear component, store, async-work, and native integration responsibilities. |
| **App Foundations** | Apply shared patterns for design, themes, localization, secure storage, notifications, branding, and cleanup. | Consistent application behavior across supported platforms. |
| **Master SDK Catalog** | Find the SDK entries and platform-specific contracts supplied by the responsible teams. | A confirmed dependency and integration path when details are available. |

**The Master SDK Catalog is a separate page in this section.** It owns SDK availability, package details, compatibility, installation, and API examples. It currently contains only the Auth SDK entry; team-provided technical details are pending.

A foundation example is not proof that a separately published internal SDK exists. Do not invent a package to connect a pattern to the catalog.

