| **Resilience** | Propagate cancellation, classify failures, and use reviewed retry/breaker policies. | Architecture: Network Resilience & Circuit Breakers. |
| **Feature flags** | Consume approved Unleash mobile integration through the defined configuration boundary. | Architecture: Feature Flags & Remote Configuration. |
| **Testing** | Add meaningful assertions and meet the defined 100% coverage scope and metrics. | Testing & Quality, including Sauce Labs. |
| **Observability** | Add permitted events and verify release-specific diagnostics. | Observability, including Datadog platform guides. |
| **AI assistance** | Use approved rules/context and review generated code and tests. | GenAI & Developer Tools. |
| **Build and distribution** | Keep project configuration compatible with approved workflows. | CI/CD and App Store & Distribution. |

A provider integration is not automatically a published internal SDK. Unleash and Datadog guidance should not appear as unconfirmed additional catalog entries.

## 8. Select and upgrade dependencies deliberately

Use the versions managed by the approved project and its dependency locks. Framework, compiler, build tools, OS targets, and native SDKs must be compatible as a set; an open-ended “version X or newer” statement is not a complete compatibility policy.

| Platform | Review when a dependency changes |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Android** | Gradle wrapper, Android Gradle Plugin, JDK, Kotlin, Compose, SDK levels, and native dependencies as applicable. |
| **iOS** | Xcode/Swift compatibility, deployment target, package resolution, concurrency requirements, and native linkage. |
| **React Native** | React Native/React, Node/package manager, native modules, Android/iOS toolchains, and release build behavior. |

Keep setup commands in Machine Setup and package-specific instructions in the SDK Catalog. For an upgrade, review migration notes, lockfile changes, initialization, account transitions, and the affected development/release configurations. Do not change versions merely to bypass an unrelated local failure.

## 9. Review before using an example in an application

Documentation examples must identify their scope: conceptual snippet, complete component, integration contract, or working feature. Check accompanying verification notes rather than assuming all samples were built on every platform.

Before adopting an example:

- Match it to the project's actual versions, architecture, and supported devices.
- Include its supporting types, resources, configuration, and dependencies.
- Replace synthetic data only through approved configuration and test-data processes.
- Verify failure, cancellation, lifecycle, and account-switch behavior.
- Run the repository's actual test and build checks.
- Report mismatches against the supplied source of truth rather than silently inventing an API or changing policy.

## 10. Implementation readiness checklist

- [ ] I know the relevant platform standard and established project stack.
- [ ] The implementation follows shared dependency and state-ownership rules.
- [ ] Required foundation behavior is defined for the target platform.
- [ ] Any SDK integration uses a real, supplied contract and approved version.
- [ ] SDK-specific details are taken from the separate catalog, with unresolved contracts treated as pending.
- [ ] Configuration, credentials, and telemetry follow the permitted data policy.
- [ ] Tests and platform checks cover the affected behavior and documented coverage requirement.
- [ ] Startup, cancellation, session changes, and cleanup have explicit ownership.
- [ ] Integration risks and remaining verification gaps are recorded accurately.

**Continue:** read the Android, iOS, or React Native Standards for your application, then apply the relevant App Foundations. Consult the Master SDK Catalog when a supplied SDK contract is available.

